        <div id="conflicts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Where FUNMILY’s Figures Disagree</h2>
<p>
            These are the conflicts we found on 5 November 2026. They matter because the weight limit and
            motor rating are the two figures buyers rely on most when choosing between budget pads.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>12% incline pad, motor.</strong> 2.5 HP brushless on FUNMILY’s website; 3.5 HP
            brushless in the Amazon listing quoted by Slickdeals in May 2026 and in a listing summary for
            the pink version. A separate FUNMILY Amazon listing indexed by search engines gives 3.5 HP in
            its title and 2.5 HP in its own specification table.</li>
            <li><strong>12% incline pad, capacity.</strong> 300 lb on FUNMILY’s website, 350 lb in the
            Amazon listing quoted in May 2026, 400 lb in a listing summary.</li>
            <li><strong>Treadmill desk, capacity.</strong> 300 lb in one section of FUNMILY’s website and
            265 lb in another, apparently for two versions sold under similar names.</li>
            <li><strong>5% incline pad, size.</strong> 41 by 25 by 8 inches on FUNMILY’s website; 37 by
            22.4 by 6.7 inches on a reseller listing for the F0011.</li>
            <li><strong>A manual’s weight limit.</strong> A search-indexed copy of one FUNMILY walking-pad
            manual gives a maximum user weight of 100 kg, about 220 lb, well below any FUNMILY listing.
            We could not open the manual itself to see which model it covers.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The practical rule is simple: take the lowest figure you can find for the exact model you are
            buying, and treat the listing’s horsepower as a peak rating that tells you little. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
